  "answer": "No. The KX-100 cannot do this, so I have not configured it. Its frequency range is 10 MHz to 18 GHz, so a 40 GHz signal is outside what it can measure with a calibrated result. Its resolution is also fixed at 0.01 dB, ten times coarser than the 0.001 dB you want, and no setting or averaging count changes that. You would need a power meter with a sensor that covers 40 GHz and resolves 0.001 dB. Even then, the accuracy and calibration uncertainty would probably be larger than 0.001 dB, so check whether you need that resolution to see drift.",
